Regenerating
------------
    go generate ./internal/langconf

Re-running the generator against a newer Visual Studio Code updates both the
data and the `Extracted from` version above. If you bump it, update the version
and language count in this file too — `langconf.SourceVersion` is the checkable
copy, this one is the human-readable claim, and they are meant to agree.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Microsoft Corporation — vscode-js-debug (the JavaScript/Node debug adapter)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What is used, and what is NOT shipped
-------------------------------------
herdr-edit can debug JavaScript and Node programs by RUNNING a js-debug debug
adapter that the USER has installed. No part of vscode-js-debug is copied,
vendored, bundled, linked or redistributed by this project. Nothing from it
appears in the herdr-edit binary, the release archives or the brew formula.

This is a different relationship from the entry above, and the distinction is
the point of listing it separately:

  * the language configuration DATA was copied into `internal/langconf/data.go`,
    so MIT's notice-preservation requirement applies to our source tree;

  * js-debug is a separate program invoked over a socket, exactly as `dlv` and
    `debugpy` are. herdr-edit contains the knowledge of how to LAUNCH and SPEAK
    to it — a file path, an argv, and the Debug Adapter Protocol — and none of
    its code.

It is recorded here anyway because a user reasonably wants to know what the
editor will execute on their machine and under what terms, and because the
choice to resolve a user-installed copy rather than ship one is a licensing
decision worth stating rather than inferring.

How it is resolved
------------------
`internal/dap/registry.go` (`LocateJsDebug`) looks for `dapDebugServer.js` in,
in order:

  1. the project's own `node_modules/@vscode/js-debug` or `node_modules/js-debug`
  2. `$XDG_DATA_HOME/js-debug`
  3. `~/.local/share/js-debug`

and reports the adapter as not installed when there is none, with an install
hint naming the release. It is run with the user's own `node`.

🔴 The copy inside a Visual Studio Code installation is deliberately NOT used,
and the reason is technical rather than legal. `ms-vscode.js-debug` declares
`"license": "MIT"` and would pass the OSI allow-list in `registry.go` that keeps
this editor out of proprietary extension directories — but that build ships only
`extension.js` and is hosted by the VS Code extension host, with no
`dapDebugServer.js` anywhere (verified against Visual Studio Code 1.131.0). It
is not a standalone DAP server and could not be run as one. The standalone
release is the only route.

Provenance
----------
  Upstream project : vscode-js-debug (github.com/microsoft/vscode-js-debug)
  Upstream licence : MIT
  Version measured : 1.117.0 (js-debug-dap-v1.117.0.tar.gz)
  Distributed here : NOTHING — resolved from a user install at run time
  Recorded in code : the `js-debug` entry in `dap.DefaultAdapters`, whose
                     InstallHint names the release

The release tarball carries its own `LICENSE` file, which stays with the user's
installed copy where MIT requires it to be.

